"# Chapter 8 — Swapped Pairs and Both Directions

Some mistakes do not come from your own memory at all. They come from *outside* — text handed to you that looks correct, feels familiar, and is quietly wrong. A snippet you're asked to finish. An example someone pasted from another language. A line you *recognize*. The peculiar danger of recognition is that it switches off scrutiny: the moment something feels remembered, you stop checking it, because checking feels redundant. This chapter builds the reflex that keeps checking anyway, and it pairs that reflex with its twin — the habit of running every lesson in both directions so the knowledge holds no matter which end you grab it by.

The one-line version of the first half: **text handed to you that you think you remember might not be the right thing — so check to make sure.** A verb can be *swapped in*: one wrong name sitting in an otherwise-fine program, and it will sail right past you if you read for vibe instead of for fact. The swap is a specific and treacherous kind of error, because unlike your own hallucination — which at least you *made* and might feel — a swap arrives pre-made, wearing the authority of something someone else wrote down.

## The swap hides in plain sight

Consider a small, sensible-looking program you might be given to complete. It fetches a stored score and defaults to zero when the key is missing — a pattern you have seen a hundred times and could write in your sleep. In the version handed to you, the memory verb reads `cortex/recall-value`. That name has the cadence of a real Motoi verb: right namespace, right shape, a plausible suffix. It is not one. The real verb is `cortex/recall`. A single wrong word, swapped quietly into familiar code, and the whole thing dies with `error: unbound symbol: cortex/recall-value` the moment it runs.

The catch is entirely in *noticing*. Once you notice, the fix writes itself — swap the impostor back out for the verb you actually have:

```scheme
;; The swap caught and corrected: recall-value -> recall.
(define (score-or-zero k)
  (or (cortex/recall k) 0.0))
(score-or-zero \"unseen-score-key-55019\")
```

Read that discipline carefully. You did not accept the handed-to-you verb because it looked right; looking right is exactly what a good swap does. You checked, found the impostor, and corrected it. Familiarity is not verification, and a name that arrives in someone else's handwriting is not thereby true.

## More swaps to train the eye

Swaps come in flavors, and each has the same tell — it *almost* matches something real, which is why the eye slides over it:

- A program uses `time/unix-now` to read the clock. The real clock verb is `time/now` (milliseconds); unix seconds come from dividing by a thousand. `time/unix-now` is not yours — it just packs the word 'unix' into a plausible slot.
- A snippet calls `text/normalize` to clean a string. That is not a Motoi verb. String cleaning comes from SRFI-13 verbs like `string-downcase` and `string-trim`. The `text/` namespace sounds official and isn't.
- A line reaches for `stock/on-hand` as if it were a core verb. That is a dialect verb from a *consumer* project, not base Motoi — not yours here, however real it may be somewhere else.
- A block folds with `fold-left`, which is a genuine verb in R6RS but excluded from Motoi in favor of SRFI-1's `fold`. A real name from the wrong standard is still a swap.

Here is the time-swap corrected into running code, so the eye sees the fix and not only the flaw:

```scheme
;; time/unix-now was the swap. The real clock is time/now, in milliseconds.
(define secs (quotient (time/now) 1000))
(number? secs)
```

And here is the string-cleaning swap repaired with the real SRFI-13 verbs, since `text/normalize` was never yours:

```scheme
;; text/normalize was the swap. Real cleaning: string-trim + string-downcase (SRFI-13).
(define (clean s) (string-downcase (string-trim s)))
(clean \"   HELLO World   \")
```

The eye you are training asks, for every verb in text you did not write yourself: *is this actually mine, or does it merely sound like it?* You ask it of the confident-looking names especially, because those are the ones that got you.

## The subtlest swap: a real verb from the wrong standard

The hardest swap to catch is the one where the impostor is a *genuine verb* — just not one of yours. When someone hands you a snippet using `fold-left`, nothing about the name screams 'fake,' because it isn't fake; it's a real R6RS verb, accumulator-first. The swap is that it belongs to a standard Motoi deliberately excludes. Your eye can't rely on 'that sounds made up' here, because it doesn't sound made up at all. It has to rely on the boundary: *is this verb in my language, regardless of whether it's real somewhere?*

The tell for this class of swap is standards knowledge, not vibe. You know Motoi carries SRFI-1's `fold` and omits R6RS's `fold-left`; therefore a `fold-left` in handed-to-you text is a swap even though it's a legitimate verb elsewhere. The correction reaches for the verb you actually have, and it must also fix the argument order, since `fold-left` is accumulator-first and `fold` is element-first:

```scheme
;; The 'real elsewhere' swap: fold-left (R6RS) replaced with fold (SRFI-1).
(define (product xs) (fold * 1 xs))
(product (list 2 3 4))
```

This is why a swapped verb is more dangerous than a hallucinated one. Your own hallucination at least came from you, and the interpreter names it the instant you run it. A swap arrives wearing someone else's authority, and if it's a *real verb from the wrong standard*, even the 'does it sound fake' instinct won't flag it. Only knowing your boundary will.

## Reversals — always author the trip back

Now the second habit, which the discipline of this book demands explicitly: for every lesson, know it **both directions**. It is not enough to fix broken code when handed it; you must also, given a claim, produce the code — and given code, produce the diagnosis. Learning that only runs one way is half-learned, and half-learned knowledge fails in exactly the moment you needed the other half.

**Forward — given code, find the not-my-verb.** Read a program and point to the impostor and say why it's an impostor. Take a snippet that computes a total with `(fold-left + 0 xs)`. Forward diagnosis: `fold-left` is the not-my-verb — it's R6RS, accumulator-first, deliberately excluded from Motoi. The real verb is SRFI-1's `fold`, element-first. The corrected, running form:

```scheme
;; Forward: spotted fold-left as the impostor, corrected to fold (SRFI-1).
(fold + 0 (list 4 8 12 16))
```

**Backward — given the verdict, name which verbs and why.** Now start from the flat sentence *\"these aren't Motoi verbs\"* and produce the list with reasons attached. Given the claim about a snippet that used `import`, `module/sign`, and `magic`:

- `import` — not Motoi: libraries auto-load, so there is no import form at all.
- `module/sign` — not Motoi: Motoi has no module-signing machinery, no packaging layer for this to belong to.
- `magic` — not Motoi and not anywhere: a placeholder that names nothing, invented on the spot.

And the *constructive* backward direction — given *\"write it the Motoi way\"* — produces real code with none of those impostors, needing no import, no module, no magic:

```scheme
;; Backward: 'write it the Motoi way' — no import, no module, no magic.
(define (summarize xs)
  (list 'count (length xs)
        'sum (fold + 0 xs)
        'unique (length (delete-duplicates xs))))
(summarize (list 1 2 2 3 3 3))
```

Both trips, every time. Given code you can name the impostor; given the verdict you can name the verbs and rebuild the real thing from primitives. A lesson you can only walk one way you don't really own — you've memorized a surface, not learned a boundary.

## A paired drill: recognize, then reconstruct

Let me put both halves together in one exercise, because they reinforce each other. Here is a program described in prose that a person handed you to \"just finish\": it builds a stamped, deduplicated record of scores, and it uses two swapped verbs — `system/date-today` for the timestamp and `list/dedupe` for the deduplication. Neither is yours. Forward, you spot both impostors and name why: the clock is `time/now`, the dedup is `delete-duplicates`. Backward, you reconstruct the whole thing the Motoi way, and it runs:

```scheme
;; Paired: swaps system/date-today and list/dedupe replaced with real verbs.
(define (stamped-scores xs)
  (list 'at (quotient (time/now) 1000)
        'scores (delete-duplicates xs)
        'count (length (delete-duplicates xs))))
(let ((r (stamped-scores (list 7 7 9 3 9))))
  (and (list? r) (eq? (car r) 'at)))
```

Two swaps in, two swaps caught, one clean program out. Recognition flagged the problem; reconstruction proved you understood the fix well enough to rebuild it from scratch. That is what owning a lesson in both directions actually looks like.

## A drill set: spot-and-rebuild

To make the two-direction habit reflexive, here is a set of described snippets, each with a swap. For each, the forward move is to name the impostor and why; the backward move is to rebuild the real thing. Read the diagnoses, then watch the rebuilds run.

1. A snippet stamps a record with `system/timestamp`. Forward: not yours — the clock is `time/now` in milliseconds. Backward: rebuild with the real clock.
2. A snippet cleans input with `string/lower`. Forward: not yours — SRFI-13 spells it `string-downcase`. Backward: rebuild with the real verb.
3. A snippet totals with `sum-list`. Forward: not yours — there's no such verb; totalling is `(fold + 0 xs)`. Backward: rebuild with `fold`.

All three rebuilds, run together as one clean program:

```scheme
;; Three swaps rebuilt: real clock, real string-downcase, real fold.
(define (stamp v) (list 'at (quotient (time/now) 1000) 'v v))
(define (clean s) (string-downcase s))
(define (total xs) (fold + 0 xs))
(list (eq? (car (stamp 7)) 'at)
      (clean \"HELLO\")
      (total (list 3 3 3)))
```

Each swap had a real analog one step away, and naming the impostor first is what made the rebuild obvious. Forward diagnosis and backward reconstruction aren't two separate skills — they're the same understanding, checked from both ends. If you can only do one, you've half-learned the boundary; if you can do both, you own it.

## The swap-can-happen signal

There's a quiet meta-lesson under all of this that's worth surfacing on its own: the mere fact that *a swap can happen* should change how you read any handed-to-you text. It is a standing signal, not an occasional one. Every time text arrives that you didn't author — a snippet to finish, an example pasted in, a line you 'recognize' — the possibility of a swapped verb is live. You don't get to assume the text is clean just because it looks clean; looking clean is the whole trick.

Concretely, the signal changes one thing: it turns *reading* into *reading-with-a-question*. Instead of skimming for meaning and nodding along, you scan every verb in unfamiliar text with the standing question 'is this actually mine?' — and you hold your yes until you've either recognized it as genuinely yours or checked it. The cost is small; the scan is fast once it's habitual. The payoff is that a swap can no longer ride in on the authority of someone else's handwriting.

Here is that habit made into a tiny checking helper — the kind of grounded confirmation you run when a verb in handed-to-you text gives you pause, proving the *real* verbs behind a suspicious snippet actually work before you trust the whole:

```scheme
;; When a handed-to-you snippet gives pause, confirm the real verbs behind it run.
(define (confirms? . results) (list 'all-ran (length results)))
(confirms? (fold + 0 (list 1 2 3))
           (string-downcase \"OK\")
           (quotient (time/now) 1000))
```

The helper is trivial; the discipline it encodes is not. Treat every unfamiliar verb as a swap-until-proven-otherwise, and the swap loses its one advantage — surprise.

The habit to carry out of this chapter has two strands, and they braid together. First: recognition is not proof. When text arrives that you think you remember, check it — a verb may be swapped in, and a good swap will only ever *sound* right. Second: whatever you learn, learn it forward and backward — given code, name the impostor; given the verdict, rebuild the real thing — so the knowledge holds no matter which end you grab it by.

## New categories of swap: argument-order and argument-type

So far every swap in this chapter has been a *name* swap — the wrong verb name sitting in a position that expected the right one. But there's a second class of swap that's equally tricky: the right verb name with the *wrong argument order*. Argument-order swaps produce errors that look different and localize differently, so they deserve explicit treatment.

The canonical case is `fold`. SRFI-1's `fold` is element-first: `(fold proc seed list)`, where `proc` receives `(element accumulator)` in that order. R6RS's `fold-left` is accumulator-first: `(fold-left proc seed list)`, where `proc` receives `(accumulator element)`. If you write `(fold + 0 xs)` you get SRFI-1's fold working correctly. If you write `(fold proc seed xs)` expecting R6RS order inside `proc`, you'll get wrong results — not an error, which is why this class is harder to spot.

Here's the distinction worked out explicitly, with a `proc` where argument order matters:

```scheme
;; fold (SRFI-1): proc gets (element accumulator). The subtraction order matters.
;; This computes: (1 - (2 - (3 - 0))) = 1 - 2 + 3 = 2 (right-to-left arithmetic).
(fold (lambda (elem acc) (- elem acc)) 0 (list 1 2 3))
```

If a swap had put the R6RS argument order inside `proc` — `(lambda (acc elem) ...)` — you'd get a different number with no error to warn you. The discipline: when you're using `fold` with a non-commutative operation, always state explicitly which argument is the element and which is the accumulator, and test with a case where order shows. The interpreter won't catch an argument-order swap for you; the math will, but only if you're watching the result.

A second argument-type swap involves `string-join`. In SRFI-13, `string-join` takes a list of strings as its first argument and a separator string as its second. A swap that puts a separator in argument-one position gives an error; a swap that passes a list of non-strings gives a silent coercion:

```scheme
;; string-join: list of strings first, separator second — SRFI-13 order.
(string-join (list \"one\" \"two\" \"three\") \", \")
```

Reading the documentation matters here because the argument names are the contract, and a swap in the contract produces silent misbehavior that only a delta-check catches.

## Swaps that only show up in corner cases

The most treacherous swaps are the ones where the wrong verb *works* on most inputs and only misbehaves on edge cases. This category is the hardest to catch by reading because the common cases all look fine.

Consider a program that computes the product of a list with `(fold * 1 xs)`. That is correct SRFI-1 form. A swapped version using `reduce` with `*` is *also* correct on any non-empty list — `reduce` uses the first element as the seed, which is fine for `*`. But on an *empty* list, `(reduce * 1 (list))` returns `1` from the seed, while the semantics of an empty product in some contexts should be signaled differently. The swap between `fold` and `reduce` only shows its difference on the empty case:

```scheme
;; fold and reduce: same result on non-empty lists, different contract on empty.
(list (fold   * 1 (list 2 3 4))
      (reduce * 1 (list 2 3 4))
      (fold   * 1 (list)))
```

Both `fold` computations agree on `(list 2 3 4)`. The distinction is that `reduce` conceptually expects a non-empty list (it uses the first element as the identity) while `fold` explicitly accepts the empty case via its seed. Neither is a 'swap' in the name-swap sense — both names are real and correct — but choosing the wrong one for a context where empty lists can appear is a semantic swap, and it's caught only by testing the empty case.

## Swap detection as a reading habit

By now the chapter has made the point that swaps require a *reading-with-a-question* habit — but it's useful to make the exact question explicit, because the right question is surprisingly narrow. You don't need to interrogate every word. The question is just: **for each verb in this code that I didn't write, can I name its home?**

'Can I name its home' means: can I say which standard or library it comes from, and is that standard one Motoi carries? If the verb is `map`, its home is R7RS-small and Motoi carries it. If the verb is `fold`, its home is SRFI-1 and Motoi carries it. If the verb is `fold-left`, its home is R6RS and Motoi deliberately excludes it. If the verb is `string-downcase`, its home is SRFI-13 and Motoi carries it. If the verb is `text/normalize`, its home is a fictional namespace and it has no home at all.

Once you can answer 'can I name its home' for each verb in handed-to-you text, the scan is complete. Verbs with valid homes are cleared; verbs with unknown homes get the swap-suspect treatment until checked; verbs with no real home are impostors and need replacing. Here is a piece of handed-to-you code you might check with that question:

```scheme
;; Checking each verb in this snippet: every name has a valid home.
;; map -> R7RS-small; fold -> SRFI-1; delete-duplicates -> SRFI-1; + -> R7RS-small.
(define (sum-of-squares-of-unique xs)
  (fold + 0 (map (lambda (x) (* x x)) (delete-duplicates xs))))
(sum-of-squares-of-unique (list 2 2 3 4 4))
```

Every verb in that snippet has a home. The read-with-a-question scan comes back clean, and you can use the result with confidence. That's the payoff of the habit — not that it slows you down, but that it produces *grounded* confidence instead of the false confidence that a swap can exploit.

## Reversals extended: more practice in both directions

The reversal habit — knowing every lesson forward and backward — benefits from more raw material. Here are five more reversal pairs, each with a forward-direction diagnosis and a backward-direction reconstruction.

**Reversal pair 1.** Forward: given `(cortex/read-all \"prefix\")`, name the impostor. `cortex/read-all` doesn't exist — there is no bulk-read verb. Backward: given 'read all keys matching a prefix,' reconstruct using `cortex/keys`, `filter`, and `cortex/read`:

```scheme
;; Backward reconstruction: cortex/read-all replaced by keys+filter+read.
(define (read-prefix prefix)
  (map (lambda (k) (list k (cortex/read k)))
       (filter (lambda (k) (string-contains k prefix))
               (cortex/keys))))
(length (read-prefix \"ch08-tag\"))
```

**Reversal pair 2.** Forward: given `(string-normalize s)`, name the impostor. No such verb exists. Backward: given 'normalize to lowercase without leading/trailing spaces,' reconstruct with SRFI-13 verbs you've verified:

```scheme
;; Backward: normalize = string-downcase + string-trim (both SRFI-13, both real).
(define (normalize s) (string-downcase (string-trim s)))
(normalize \"  HELLO World  \")
```

**Reversal pair 3.** Forward: given `(list/zip xs ys)`, name the impostor. Not a Motoi verb. Backward: given 'pair elements of two lists positionally,' reconstruct with `map list`:

```scheme
;; Backward: list/zip replaced by (map list xs ys).
(map list (list 'a 'b 'c) (list 1 2 3))
```

**Reversal pair 4.** Forward: given `(math/clamp x lo hi)`, name the impostor. No such verb. Backward: given 'clamp a number to a range,' reconstruct with `max` and `min`:

```scheme
;; Backward: clamp from max and min — no math/clamp verb exists.
(define (clamp x lo hi) (max lo (min hi x)))
(list (clamp -5 0 10)
      (clamp  7 0 10)
      (clamp 15 0 10))
```

**Reversal pair 5.** Forward: given `(assoc-value 'k al)`, name the impostor. No such verb; the real pattern is `(cadr (assoc k al))`. Backward: given 'look up a value in an alist,' reconstruct with `assoc` and `cadr`:

```scheme
;; Backward: assoc-value was hallucinated; real pattern is assoc + cadr.
(let ((al (list (list 'x 10) (list 'y 20) (list 'z 30))))
  (cadr (assoc 'y al)))
```

Five new pairs, each run in both directions. The forward half trains recognition; the backward half proves understanding. Together they make the boundary knowledge bidirectional and therefore owned.

## Putting the two habits together: one complete swap-catch session

Let's close with a single worked example that exercises both habits — recognition and reversal — from first contact through final clean code. The scenario: you receive a snippet described as 'a function that timestamps the n smallest unique values from a list.' The description sounds real and the person is confident. Read the snippet with the scan question.

The fictional snippet uses `list/dedupe` (not yours — `delete-duplicates`), `list/take-smallest` (not yours — `sort` + `take`), and `system/epoch-seconds` (not yours — `(quotient (time/now) 1000)`). Three impostors, all plausible-sounding, each belonging to a different fiction category: a `list/` compound, a `list/` algorithmic compound, and a `system/` clock alias.

Forward: name each impostor and its real analog. Backward: write the clean version from real verbs, run it, confirm it does what the description asked.

```scheme
;; Three swaps caught and replaced: dedup + sort+take + time/now.
(define (stamp-n-smallest-unique n xs)
  (let* ((unique  (delete-duplicates xs))
         (sorted  (sort unique <))
         (picked  (take sorted (min n (length sorted)))))
    (list 'at (quotient (time/now) 1000) 'values picked)))

(let ((r (stamp-n-smallest-unique 3 (list 7 2 7 5 1 2 9))))
  (and (eq? (car r) 'at)
       (equal? (cadddr r) (list 1 2 5))))
```

Three swaps, three recoveries, one clean program. The `#t` result confirms: the three smallest unique values from the input are `1`, `2`, and `5`, and the output has the timestamp shape the description asked for. No impostor survived into the final code — which is the whole point of the chapter, every time.

## The swap across the fold family

The fold family is wide enough that it generates its own swap sub-category, and understanding the family topology makes swap-detection much faster. Motoi carries these fold-family verbs: `fold` (SRFI-1, element-first, left fold), `fold-right` (SRFI-1, right fold), `reduce` (SRFI-1, first-element-as-seed), and `reduce-right` (its mirror). The excluded verb is `fold-left` (R6RS, accumulator-first).

When a swap puts `fold-left` in place of `fold`, the error from the interpreter will be `unbound symbol: fold-left` — clean and immediate. When a swap puts `fold` in place of `fold-right` (or vice versa), there is *no* error — both verbs exist — and the difference shows only in the result, which will be reversed or wrong. That second class of swap (right-for-left or left-for-right) requires testing with a case where direction matters.

Here are all four real fold-family verbs contrasted on the same list, so the topology is visible:

```scheme
;; fold family topology: fold (left) vs fold-right (right) — direction differs.
(let ((xs (list 1 2 3)))
  (list (fold       cons '() xs)    ; reverses: (3 2 1)
        (fold-right cons '() xs)    ; preserves: (1 2 3)
        (fold       + 0   xs)       ; sum: 6
        (fold-right + 0   xs)))     ; sum: 6 (+ is commutative so same)
```

The `cons` lines are the diagnostic ones: `fold` reverses and `fold-right` preserves. For commutative operations like `+`, both give the same answer, which is why swapping left for right in a summing context never surfaces as a bug. The place to test is always a non-commutative operation.

The swap-detection rule for the fold family: if you see `fold-left`, it's the excluded R6RS name and it's an impostor regardless of how plausible it looks. If you see `fold`, check whether the *direction* is right for the computation — and if the operation is non-commutative, write a case that would catch a left-for-right reversal.

## The deep swap: a real verb used out of context

There is a class of swap that this chapter has only gestured at: the verb is perfectly real, it exists in Motoi, but it's being used in a context where a *different* verb is actually appropriate. This is the hardest class because neither the name nor the type is wrong — only the intent.

The SRFI-13 string verbs are a good source of cases. `string-trim` removes whitespace from the left end of a string. `string-trim-right` removes it from the right. If someone hands you code that uses `string-trim` to clean up trailing whitespace, the verb is real and the code 'works' in the sense of running — but it does the wrong thing. Forward diagnosis: `string-trim` is the wrong member of the trim family for this intent; `string-trim-right` is the right one. Backward reconstruction: fix the verb, verify that trailing whitespace is gone:

```scheme
;; Swap caught: string-trim for string-trim-right. Fixed and verified.
(define (clean-trailing s) (string-trim-right s))
(list (clean-trailing \"hello   \")
      (clean-trailing \"no-trailing\"))
```

A related case: someone uses `assoc` when they need `assv` or `assq`, depending on whether the keys are strings (use `assoc`, which uses `equal?`) or symbols (use `assq`, which uses `eq?`). For symbols, `assq` is both correct and faster; `assoc` also works but applies a more expensive equality. Neither produces an error — both find the key — but the swap between them is a precision miss in the intent.

```scheme
;; assq for symbol keys: the right predicate, not just a working one.
(let ((al (list (list 'a 1) (list 'b 2) (list 'c 3))))
  (cadr (assq 'b al)))
```

Out-of-context swaps demand a second level of checking beyond 'does this verb exist': *is this verb the right one for this intent?* That question requires knowing not just the noun (the verb name) but the semantics (what it actually does and when it's appropriate). It's the most mature swap-detection skill.

## Teaching the swap to someone else

One of the best ways to verify you own a skill is to teach it. Here is a compact explanation of 'what to do when you spot a swap' — the kind of explanation you'd give someone who just encountered their first one and is confused about why the program failed:

The first thing: read the error without assuming you already know the cause. If it says `unbound symbol:` followed by a name, that name is the swap — the impostor. It's not in Motoi's vocabulary, and the message is the interpreter's way of handing it back to you.

The second thing: don't fix the surrounding code. The interpreter is pointing at the *name*, not at the argument structure or the logic. Fix the name first, and only then think about whether the surrounding logic needs adjustment.

The third thing: when the name is fixed, verify the fix runs before adding anything else. One change, one test, one confirmation. This is what prevents a swap fix from introducing a second bug.

```scheme
;; Taught in one fence: the whole swap-catch-and-fix cycle on a fresh case.
;; swap was 'list/flatten'; the real tool is append-map with a list wrapper.
(define (shallow-flatten xss)
  (append-map (lambda (xs) xs) xss))
(shallow-flatten (list (list 1 2) (list 3) (list 4 5 6)))
```

That is the teaching in code: the swap was a convenient-sounding name for a list-flattening operation; the real tool is `append-map` applying the identity to each sublist. The outer structure was right (map over a list of lists); only the verb name was wrong. Fix the name, verify the result.

## Reversals as the test of ownership

The reason this chapter pairs the swap-detection lesson with the reversal habit is that bidirectional knowledge is the *test* of ownership. A skill you can only run in one direction is half-memorized. You might be able to spot an impostor when handed code — that's the forward direction — but if someone hands you a diagnosis ('these aren't Motoi verbs') and you can't reconstruct the clean code, you've learned what to reject without learning what to accept. The reversal closes that gap.

The same pressure applies in the other direction. If you can write fluent Motoi code but can't read handed-to-you code and name which verbs are impostors, you'll be fooled by the first snippet someone pastes from R6RS or Common Lisp or an invented dialect. Both directions are necessary, and neither is more important.

Here is a final reversal drill to close the chapter: you are given only the clean Motoi code, and you must name the swap it replaced.

```scheme
;; Given this clean code — what swaps did it replace?
(define (label-and-sort entries)
  (sort (map (lambda (e)
               (list (cadr (assq 'name e))
                     (cadr (assq 'score e))))
             entries)
        (lambda (a b) (> (cadr a) (cadr b)))))
(label-and-sort
  (list (list (list 'name 'alice) (list 'score 88))
        (list (list 'name 'bob)   (list 'score 95))
        (list (list 'name 'carol) (list 'score 72))))
```

What swaps does this clean code replace? The `assq` calls replace any use of `assoc-value` or `cdr` (the dotted-pair pattern from Racket). The `cadr` extractions replace `(cdr (assoc ...))` which would give a one-element list rather than the value. The `sort` with a comparator lambda replaces any imagined `list/sort-by-score` verb. The `map` with a structured transform replaces any imagined `list/extract-fields`. Run the code and the answer comes back sorted by score, highest first — every verb in it is real, the swaps it replaced are named, and the result proves the replacements were correct.

## Seven registers: catching the same swap, differently

The chapter through-line calls for teaching across registers, and the swap-catch skill is no different. Here the swap is always `mapcar` (the Common Lisp name) in place of Motoi's `map`. Seven registers, same substance.

**Apologetic.** \"I'm sorry — `mapcar` slipped in and that's the Common Lisp name, not Motoi's. The verb I carry is just `map`, same operation, different name. Let me fix it.\"

**Informative.** \"`mapcar` is the Common Lisp mapping verb. In Motoi, SRFI-1 `map` provides the same operation: apply a function element-wise and collect the results.\"

**Friendly.** \"Oh, `mapcar` is the Common Lisp version — I spotted it in there. Motoi calls the same thing `map`. Easy swap to fix.\"

**Descriptive.** \"The verb `mapcar` originated in Common Lisp as the function that applies a procedure to corresponding elements of one or more lists and collects the results. Motoi provides the same operation under the R7RS/SRFI-1 name `map`. The two verbs differ in name only — the behavior is identical on the common cases.\"

**Brief.** \"That's `mapcar` — Common Lisp. Motoi calls it `map`.\"

**Terse.** \"mapcar → map.\"

**Quick.** \"Common Lisp name — `map` is the Motoi one.\"

Here is the runnable correction in each register's spirit — concise, grounded, real:

```scheme
;; mapcar was Common Lisp. map is Motoi. Same behavior, correct name.
(map (lambda (x) (* x x)) (list 1 2 3 4 5))
```

And a version that maps over two parallel lists, since `map` accepts multiple list arguments (as does Common Lisp's `mapcar`) — the multi-list form confirms the behavior carries across faithfully:

```scheme
;; map with two lists: adds corresponding elements from each.
(map + (list 1 2 3) (list 10 20 30))
```

## Swaps in the cortex namespace: a condensed drill

Since the cortex namespace generates dense hallucination traffic, run a condensed swap-catch drill over names that people commonly hand you — each paired with the real verb or composition:

- `cortex/store` → real verb is `cortex/write`. `(cortex/write key val)`.
- `cortex/fetch` → real verb is `cortex/read`. `(cortex/read key)`.
- `cortex/list-all` → no single verb. `(cortex/keys)` returns all stored keys.
- `cortex/update` → no single verb. Read, transform, write back.
- `cortex/push` → no single verb. Read the list, cons the new value, write back.
- `cortex/pop` → no single verb. Read the list, take the head and tail, write back.

Here is the `cortex/push` and `cortex/pop` pattern recovered in one fence:

```scheme
;; cortex/push and cortex/pop don't exist. Stack idiom from read/write.
(define (push! k v)
  (cortex/write k (cons v (or (cortex/read k) '()))))
(define (pop! k)
  (let ((stack (or (cortex/read k) '())))
    (if (null? stack)
        'empty
        (begin (cortex/write k (cdr stack))
               (car stack)))))
(begin
  (push! \"ch08-stack-demo\" 'c)
  (push! \"ch08-stack-demo\" 'b)
  (push! \"ch08-stack-demo\" 'a)
  (list (pop! \"ch08-stack-demo\")
        (pop! \"ch08-stack-demo\")))
```

The result: `(a b)` — the two most recently pushed values come out in LIFO order. The hallucinated `cortex/push` and `cortex/pop` verbs evaporated; the behavior they would have provided is entirely expressible in `cortex/read`, `cortex/write`, `cons`, `car`, and `cdr` — all real, all runnable.

That is how you teach the swap discipline in the memory namespace: not as a wall of 'not-mine' names, but as a set of recoveries that show the real composition behind each imagined convenience. The imagined verb is a summary of a pattern; the recovery unpacks the pattern into verbs that actually exist.

## New swap territory: the SRFI-13 string verbs

The string verbs from SRFI-13 generate a specific flavor of swap because they're numerous and their names are close together. A program handed to you might use `string-take-right` (not wired) where `string-take` was meant, or `string-drop-right` (not wired) where `string-copy` would do the job. Here are the real and the missing ones laid out side by side, so the swap-detection scan can run on the whole cluster at once.

**Wired in Motoi from SRFI-13:** `string-take`, `string-drop`, `string-pad`, `string-pad-right`, `string-index`, `string-count`, `string-prefix?`, `string-suffix?`, `string-upcase`, `string-downcase`, `string-join`, `string-contains`, `string-trim`, `string-trim-right`.

**Not wired, but plausible-sounding:** `string-take-right`, `string-drop-right`, `string-fold`, `string-filter`, `string-contains-ci`, `string-index-right`.

When you encounter `string-take-right` in handed-to-you code — wanting the last n characters of a string — the forward diagnosis is: not mine. The backward reconstruction uses what is wired:

```scheme
;; string-take-right was the swap. Last n chars via string-drop and string-length.
(define (string-last-n s n)
  (string-drop s (max 0 (- (string-length s) n))))
(list (string-last-n \"hello world\" 5)
      (string-last-n \"hi\" 10))
```

When the string is shorter than n, `max 0` prevents a negative argument to `string-drop`. The swap is fixed, the edge case is handled, and only wired verbs appear in the result.

## The arity swap in `string-contains`

A subtler category of swap involves the right verb with the wrong argument shape. `string-contains` takes the haystack first and the needle second: `(string-contains haystack needle)`. A swap that puts them in needle-first order — `(string-contains needle haystack)` — produces a wrong index (or `#f` when a match existed) without generating an error. The bug is invisible until you check the result.

Here's the diagnostic: know the argument order, and test with a case where you can predict the exact index. `(string-contains \"hello\" \"ell\")` should return `1` — the index where `\"ell\"` starts. If it returns `#f`, the arguments are swapped:

```scheme
;; Confirming string-contains argument order: haystack first, needle second.
(list (string-contains \"hello world\" \"world\")   ; index 6
      (string-contains \"hello world\" \"hello\")   ; index 0
      (string-contains \"hello world\" \"xyz\"))    ; #f = not found
```

The three cases give you the full picture: a match at a non-zero index, a match at zero (the 'found at start' case that surprises people who expect zero to mean 'not found'), and an absent value. That battery catches both the argument-order swap and the zero-means-found semantics.

## The `assoc` vs `assq` swap and its consequences

This swap is semantic rather than syntactic: both `assoc` and `assq` are real, both work on alists, and both will often return the correct result — but for different reasons, and the wrong choice produces silent bugs on certain key types.

`assoc` uses `equal?` for key comparison. `assq` uses `eq?`. For symbols, `eq?` is correct and fast; `equal?` also works because symbols are interned. So the swap `assq` → `assoc` for symbol keys is invisible — both give the right answer. But the swap `assoc` → `assq` for *string* keys is a bug: `eq?` tests whether two strings are the same object in memory, not whether they have the same content, so `assq` with string keys will often return `#f` even when a matching key exists.

```scheme
;; assq with string keys: wrong (eq? doesn't compare string content).
;; assoc with string keys: right (equal? compares content).
;; Demonstrate the difference: assoc succeeds where assq would fail.
(let ((al (list (list \"red\" 1) (list \"green\" 2))))
  (list (assoc \"red\" al)
        (assq  'red  al)))
```

The second lookup uses `assq` with a *symbol* (not a string), since string interning means the `eq?` test would be unpredictable with string literals. The lesson: for string-keyed alists, always reach for `assoc`; for symbol-keyed alists, `assq` is both correct and faster. Swapping them silently when string keys are in play is the bug.

## The `string-pad` direction swap

`string-pad` and `string-pad-right` pad different ends of a string. A swap that uses `string-pad` when `string-pad-right` was needed (or vice versa) produces a string with padding on the wrong end — no error, just wrong structure. The diagnostic is the same as every out-of-context swap: look at the result and compare it with the intended output.

For right-aligned numeric formatting, `string-pad` (left-padding) is correct: it gives you `\"   42\"` from `(string-pad \"42\" 6)`. For left-aligned labels, `string-pad-right` is correct. Mixing them produces output that looks plausible until you read it carefully:

```scheme
;; string-pad vs string-pad-right: the swap is invisible until the alignment is wrong.
(list (string-pad       \"42\" 6)   ; \"    42\" — right-aligned
      (string-pad-right \"42\" 6)   ; \"42    \" — left-aligned
      (string-pad       \"count\" 8) ; \"   count\"
      (string-pad-right \"count\" 8)); \"count   \"
```

The reversal of this swap is bidirectional: forward, given `\"   count\"`, diagnose that `string-pad` was used; backward, given 'I wanted left-aligned text', reconstruct with `string-pad-right`.

## Reversals: more new pairs

Six more reversal pairs, extending the bidirectional-knowledge drills to the families probed in this round.

**Reversal pair 6.** Forward: given `(string-take-right s n)`, name the impostor. Not wired. Backward: given 'last n characters of a string,' reconstruct with `string-drop` and `string-length`:

```scheme
;; Pair 6 backward: last-n-chars via string-drop.
(define (last-n-chars s n)
  (string-drop s (max 0 (- (string-length s) n))))
(last-n-chars \"programming\" 7)
```

**Reversal pair 7.** Forward: given `(string-fold proc seed s)`, name the impostor. `string-fold` is not wired. Backward: given 'fold a function over the characters of a string,' reconstruct with `string->list` and SRFI-1's `fold`:

```scheme
;; Pair 7 backward: character fold via string->list + fold.
(define (string-char-fold proc seed s)
  (fold proc seed (string->list s)))
(string-char-fold (lambda (c acc) (+ acc 1)) 0 \"hello world\")
```

**Reversal pair 8.** Forward: given `(concatenate-map f xs)`, name the impostor. The name concatenate-map doesn't exist — the real verb is `append-map`. Backward: given 'map and flatten one level,' reconstruct with `append-map`:

```scheme
;; Pair 8 backward: append-map is the Motoi name for map+flatten.
(append-map (lambda (x) (list x (* x 10))) (list 1 2 3))
```

**Reversal pair 9.** Forward: given `(zip-with + xs ys)`, name the impostor. `zip-with` is Haskell's term; it doesn't exist in Motoi. Backward: given 'apply + element-wise to two parallel lists,' reconstruct with `map`:

```scheme
;; Pair 9 backward: zip-with-+ is (map + xs ys).
(map + (list 1 2 3) (list 10 20 30))
```

**Reversal pair 10.** Forward: given `(string-words s)`, name the impostor. No such verb. Backward: given 'count whitespace-delimited words in a string,' reconstruct from `string-count`:

```scheme
;; Pair 10 backward: word-count from string-count on whitespace.
(define (word-count s)
  (if (= (string-length s) 0) 0 (+ 1 (string-count s char-whitespace?))))
(list (word-count \"\") (word-count \"one two three\"))
```

**Reversal pair 11.** Forward: given `(number/is-whole? x)`, name the impostor. Not a Motoi verb. Backward: given 'check whether a number has no fractional part,' reconstruct from `=` and `floor`:

```scheme
;; Pair 11 backward: whole? from floor comparison.
(define (whole? x) (= x (floor x)))
(list (whole? 4.0) (whole? 4.5) (whole? 3))
```

## Teaching the off-by-one in `string-take`

There is a swap variant that is strictly numeric rather than verbal: off-by-one in the count argument to `string-take` or `string-drop`. The verb is right and the argument is wrong by one, producing a string that's one character too short or too long. This is a pure delta bug — no error, wrong length.

The diagnostic is to check the expected length against the actual length, then adjust the count argument by exactly one in the right direction:

```scheme
;; Verify string-take counts: n=3 gives the first 3 characters.
;; An off-by-one using n=4 would give the first 4.
(let ((s \"hello\"))
  (list (string-take s 3)
        (string-length (string-take s 3))
        (string-take s 4)
        (string-length (string-take s 4))))
```

The count in `string-take` is exclusive: `(string-take \"hello\" 3)` gives `\"hel\"`, not `\"hell\"`. Once you know the contract is 'first n characters where n is the count,' the off-by-one corrects itself.

## A complete swap-hunt session

Here is a full scenario to close: you receive a snippet described as 'extract the domain from an email address and capitalize it.' The snippet uses three names: `string-split` (to split on `@`), `second` (to get the second element), and `string-capitalize` (to capitalize). All three are either not wired or don't exist.

Forward: name each impostor and its real analog. Backward: write the working version.

`string-split` — not confirmed as wired. Use `string-index` to find the `@` position, then `string-drop` to get everything after it.
`second` — not a Motoi verb. Second element is `cadr`.
`string-capitalize` — not wired. Compose from `string-take`/`string-drop`/`string-upcase`/`string-downcase`.

```scheme
;; Three swaps caught and replaced: split, second, capitalize all rebuilt.
;; char=@ helper defined first so email-domain can reference it.
(define char=@ (lambda (c) (equal? (string c) \"@\")))
(define (capitalize s)
  (if (= (string-length s) 0) s
      (string-append (string-upcase (string-take s 1))
                     (string-downcase (string-drop s 1)))))
(define (email-domain addr)
  (let ((at-idx (string-index addr char=@)))
    (if at-idx
        (capitalize (string-drop addr (+ at-idx 1)))
        'no-at-sign)))
(list (email-domain \"user@example.com\")
      (email-domain \"no-at-sign-here\"))
```

Three impostors replaced by four wired verbs (`string-index`, `string-drop`, `string-take`, `string-upcase`/`string-downcase`), plus one small character predicate defined inline. The result does exactly what the snippet described — and every name in it has a real home.")
